    "body": "`encrypt` initializes its ciphers with `getKey()`, but CipherSpec does not recognize that value as coming from an accepted key producer. CipherSpec also rejects the existing `AES/CBC/PKCS7` transformation; this change leaves that transformation unchanged.\n\nI now create keys with the explicit `AndroidKeyStore` provider and use one shared alias for creation and retrieval. Existing aliases are retrieved without generating a new key, while first use still creates the same `secret` alias. This is safe because it preserves the stored alias and key-creation parameters.\n\nI found this while running the app under RVSec, a runtime checker for how apps use the Java crypto and TLS APIs.\n\nThe project builds with this change (`assembleDebug`).\n\nI'm a Computer Engineering student at the University of Brasília (UnB), working on a research project on Android app analysis. Happy to adjust this to the project's conventions.\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n<!-- coderabbit-ai-generated:v1 -->\n\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **Bug Fixes**\n  * Improved handling of encryption keys, allowing compatible keys already stored on the device to be reused. Unexpected key types are now reported as errors rather than being treated as encryption keys. Encryption and decryption behavior remains unchanged.\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->",
